<%# ONE DRAW, IN SEQUENCE: everything that happened between a vantage and the
    places the model said lie beyond it.

    ONE COLUMN, TOP TO BOTTOM, and every step marked with WHO decided it -- purple
    for a model call, teal for the app deciding from records it already holds.
    `LabHelper`'s header has the captain's reason: *"It's still hard for me to
    hold in my head the full sequence of everything that happens."*

    AND THE ORDER IS INVERTED FROM THE REALIZATION LAB'S. There, the room's prose
    is the subject and the ways out are step five of nine. Here the exits answer
    IS the subject and the room's description is the context it was written in --
    so the detail call is collapsed and the exits prompt, the answer and what the
    engine did with each pick get the room.

    STEP SIX IS THE ONE THAT DOES NOT EXIST ON THE OTHER PAGE, and it is the step
    the captain asked for by asking for this space: what the engine ROLLED from
    each pick, or that it threw the pick away. Three quarters of the bands in the
    stored baseline disappear there. %>
<%= render "lab/styles" %>
<%= render "lab/exits/styles" %>

<% reading = @sample.reading %>

<h1>draw #<%= @sample.id %></h1>
<p class="nav">
  <%# THE ARROW AS ITSELF RATHER THAN AS `&larr;`.html_safe, unlike the three
      other back-links in this namespace: this one carries a VANTAGE'S NAME,
      which is text a person typed, and `html_safe` on a string interpolating it
      would hand it to the page unescaped. The others label a fixed page. %>
  <%= link_to "← #{@vantage.name}", lab_exits_vantage_path(@vantage) %>
  &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <%= @vantage.world %>
  &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <%= @sample.created_at.strftime("%d %b %Y %H:%M") %>
  <% if @sample.row["answered_by"].present? %>
    &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <%= @sample.row["answered_by"] %>
  <% end %>
  <% if @sample.row["input_tokens"].present? %>
    &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <%= @sample.row["input_tokens"] %> in /
    <%= @sample.row["output_tokens"] %> out
  <% end %>
  <% if @sample.row["seconds"].present? %>
    &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <%= number_with_precision(@sample.row["seconds"], precision: 1) %>s
  <% end %>
</p>

<% if @sample.failed? %>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3 class="warn">the call failed</h3>
    <p><%= @sample.error %></p>
    <p class="note">
      A failed call is still a sample: a provider dropping one call in a hundred
      must not cost the whole look, and a refusal is the one failure that is about
      the prompt. It is out of every denominator.
    </p>
  </div>
<% end %>

<h2>the sequence</h2>

<div class="step engine">
  <h3>1 &mdash; the vantage, stood up <%= lab_who(:engine) %></h3>
  <div class="fields">
    <div class="k">name</div>
    <div class="v"><%= reading.room %></div>
    <div class="k">teaser</div>
    <div class="v"><%= @vantage.teaser %></div>
    <div class="k">danger</div>
    <div class="v"><%= debug_value(reading.facts["danger"]) %></div>
    <div class="k">off the books</div>
    <div class="v">
      <% if @vantage.absent_names.empty? %>
        <span class="warn">nothing</span>
        <span class="absent">
          &mdash; so the prompt below offers every place this world holds as
          somewhere to reuse, and a pick about one of them decides nothing
        </span>
      <% else %>
        <%# A LITERAL SEPARATOR AND NO `html_safe`, which is `debug/show.html.erb`'s
            precedent and here it is load-bearing: these are names a person TYPED,
            so `html_safe` on a string built from them is an unescaped attribute
            and Brakeman says so in a high-confidence warning. Escape the names;
            spell the middle dot as itself. %>
        <%= @vantage.absent_names.join(" · ") %>
        <span class="absent">&mdash; destroyed before the call, so the model has a reason to invent</span>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="note">
    Stood up by <code>Location::Generator.create_stub!</code> through
    <code>Eval::Realization::Stage</code>, inside a transaction that has already
    been rolled back. There is no room, no cast and no story left behind: this row
    is all that survived, and it survived complete.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="step engine">
  <h3>2 &mdash; what the world held before the call <%= lab_who(:engine) %></h3>
  <div class="fields">
    <div class="k">places on the books</div>
    <div class="v">
      <% if reading.places.empty? %>
        <span class="absent">(none)</span>
      <% else %>
        <% reading.places.each do |place| %>
          <%= place["name"] %><%= tag.span(" (already written)", class: "absent") if place["realized"] %><br>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
    <div class="k">already reachable</div>
    <div class="v"><%= reading.reachable.join(", ").presence || tag.span("(nothing)", class: "absent") %></div>
    <div class="k">ways out left</div>
    <div class="v">
      <%= reading.exit_allowance %> of <%= Location::ExitsSchema::MAX_EXITS %>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step model">
  <h3>3 &mdash; the detail call <%= lab_who(:model) %></h3>
  <p class="note">
    The prose is the context the exits answer was written in, not the subject of
    this page &mdash; the exits prompt says <em>"consistent with the description
    you just wrote"</em> twice, which is why the two calls are one conversation
    and why there is no cheaper exits-only sample to buy.
  </p>
  <details class="exchange">
    <summary>the description and the lore this vantage got</summary>
    <div class="body">
      <%= debug_prose(reading.description) %>
      <%= debug_prose(reading.detail["lore"].to_s) %>
    </div>
  </details>
  <%= render "lab/samples/exchange", call: "detail", sample: @sample %>
</div>

<div class="step model">
  <h3>4 &mdash; the exits prompt, as sent <%= lab_who(:model) %></h3>
  <p class="note">
    Read off the <code>messages</code> rows and never rebuilt. The
    <code>inside</code> sentence and the <em>no article</em> clause are in this
    text, and reading them beside a wrong pick is the difference between a
    diagnosis and a complaint. It is also the one thing this lab may not edit
    &mdash; see <code>Lab::Exits</code>'s header.
  </p>
  <%= render "lab/samples/exchange", call: "exits", sample: @sample %>
</div>

<div class="step model">
  <h3>5 &mdash; the answer, one block per place named <%= lab_who(:model) %></h3>
  <% if !@sample.answered? %>
    <p class="absent">
      (no exits call was answered &mdash;
      <%= reading.asked_for_exits? ? "the answer named nothing" : "this vantage was already at its exit cap, so nothing was asked" %>.
      Out of every denominator.)
    </p>
  <% else %>
    <% inside_expected = @vantage.judgements.index_by(&:name_key) %>
    <% @sample.named_places.each do |place| %>
      <% judgement = inside_expected[place.key] %>
      <div class="place <%= "reaching" if place.inside? && place.new_place? %>">
        <h3><%= place.name %></h3>
        <p><em><%= place.teaser %></em></p>
        <div class="fields">
          <div class="k">inside</div>
          <div class="v">
            <%= lab_pick(place.band, judgement&.expects(Lab::Exits.pick("inside"))) %>
            <% unless place.pick_made? %>
              <span class="absent">
                &mdash; no band at all, so the engine took the quietest option.
                <code>inside_declined</code>, which is a decision not made rather
                than a decision to have none
              </span>
            <% end %>
          </div>
          <div class="k">population</div>
          <div class="v">
            <%= lab_pick(place.population, judgement&.expects(Lab::Exits.pick("population"))) %>
          </div>
          <div class="k">how far</div>
          <div class="v"><%= place.distance %> &middot; <%= place.travel_method %></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
</div>

<div class="step engine">
  <h3>6 &mdash; what the engine did with each pick <%= lab_who(:engine) %></h3>
  <p class="note">
    The step this lab exists for. A band handed to a place the world already held
    is <strong>discarded</strong>: <code>Location::Generator#connect_exit!</code>
    only passes one to <code>create_stub!</code> when the place does not exist. On
    the stored baseline, two thirds of the bands given went this way.
  </p>
  <% if @sample.answered? %>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>place</th><th>the band</th><th>what the engine did</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <% @sample.named_places.each do |place| %>
            <tr>
              <td><%= place.name %></td>
              <td><%= place.band %></td>
              <td>
                <% if !place.new_place? %>
                  <span class="absent">
                    the place already existed, so the edge was written and
                    <%= place.inside? ? "the band was thrown away" : "there was no band to use" %>
                  </span>
                <% elsif place.inside? %>
                  <span class="pick hit">
                    opened it and rolled a footprint inside
                    <code><%= place.band %></code>
                    (<%= Location::Parameters::INSIDE.fetch(place.band).to_a.minmax.join("--") %> paces a side),
                    so <code>Location#place?</code> is now true of it and its
                    inside is laid out when somebody walks in
                  </span>
                <% else %>
                  <span class="absent">opened it with no extent &mdash; open ground</span>
                <% end %>
              </td>
            </tr>
          <% end %>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="fields">
      <div class="k">places the engine opened</div>
      <div class="v">
        <%= reading.new_places.join(", ").presence || tag.span("(none -- every name restated a place the world already had)", class: "warn") %>
      </div>
      <div class="k">insides given / reaching</div>
      <div class="v">
        <%= @sample.insides_given.size %> /
        <span class="<%= @sample.insides_reaching.any? ? "pick hit" : "pick miss" %>">
          <%= @sample.insides_reaching.size %>
        </span>
      </div>
    </div>
  <% else %>
    <p class="absent">(nothing was answered, so there was nothing to act on)</p>
  <% end %>
</div>

<div class="step engine">
  <h3>7 &mdash; what the checks read <%= lab_who(:engine) %></h3>
  <% if @sample.flags.empty? %>
    <p class="absent">(nothing flagged)</p>
  <% else %>
    <% @sample.flags.each do |flag| %>
      <p><span class="warn"><%= flag.code %></span> <%= flag.evidence %></p>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
  <p class="note">
    <code>Eval::Realization::Scorer</code>'s own checks, run on this stored row.
    They are the bench's reading and not this lab's &mdash; the lab's reading is
    the rate against what you said the picks should be.
  </p>
</div>

<h2>your verdict on the set of ways out</h2>
<p class="note">
  This is about the answer as a whole: the right number of ways out, no invented
  passage out of a dead end, a reason to prefer one over another. Every claim
  about a <em>particular</em> place is a judgement, and those are on
  <%= link_to "the vantage's page", lab_exits_vantage_path(@vantage, anchor: "places") %>
  &mdash; one verdict on four picks cannot say which was wrong.
</p>
<%= form_with url: lab_exits_sample_path(@sample), method: :patch, class: "lab-form" do %>
  <div class="panel">
    <div class="fields">
      <div class="k">verdict</div>
      <div class="v">
        <% Lab::Exits::Sample::VERDICTS.each do |verdict| %>
          <label class="lab-choice">
            <%= radio_button_tag :verdict, verdict, @sample.verdict == verdict %>
            <%= debug_verdict(verdict) %>
          </label>
        <% end %>
        <label class="lab-choice">
          <%= radio_button_tag :verdict, "", @sample.verdict.blank? %>
          <span class="absent">(none)</span>
        </label>
      </div>

      <div class="k">
        which part
        <br><span class="absent">(only on a weak or a bad; none of them is a complete answer)</span>
      </div>
      <div class="v">
        <% Lab::Exits::Sample::ASPECTS.each do |aspect| %>
          <label class="lab-choice">
            <%= check_box_tag "aspects[]", aspect, @sample.aspect?(aspect), id: "aspect_#{aspect}" %>
            <%= aspect.tr("_", " ") %>
          </label>
        <% end %>
      </div>

      <div class="k">note</div>
      <div class="v"><%= text_area_tag :note, @sample.note, rows: 3, cols: 64 %></div>
    </div>
    <p><%= submit_tag @sample.verdict? ? "amend the verdict" : "record the verdict" %></p>
  </div>
<% end %>
